.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt ACPI 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm acpi
.Nd the firmware's tables and methods, the interrupt controllers, the tick and the processors
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /proc/interrupts ,
.Pa /proc/acpi/call
.Pp
Kernel command line:
.Ql maxcpus=N ,
.Ql noapic ,
.Ql nox2apic ,
.Ql rach2.noacpica ,
.Ql rach2.nomsi ,
.Ql rach2.nosci ,
.Ql rach2.resetskip=N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
At boot the kernel reads the machine's ACPI tables: the root system
description pointer that the boot loader gives or that the BIOS left in
its data area, the RSDT or the XSDT it leads to, and from that the MADT,
the FADT, the HPET table, the MCFG and the FADT's DSDT.
A table whose length or checksum fails is left out and logged.
The one value this reader takes from the DSDT is the sleep type pair of
the soft-off state,
.Ql \e_S5 ,
found by a search of its bytes.
.Pp
Once the scheduler runs, ACPICA, Intel's interpreter of ACPI, loads the
DSDT and the SSDTs into its namespace, puts the machine in ACPI mode, runs
the objects'
.Ql _STA
and
.Ql _INI
methods, tells the firmware the interrupt controller in use through
.Ql \e_PIC ,
and enables the general-purpose events that have methods.
It takes the system control interrupt, the SCI, whose line the kernel
keeps masked until then.
Work ACPICA defers, such as a notify, runs on the kernel thread
.Ql kacpid ,
made once init has pid 1.
.Pp
The embedded controller, the device
.Ql PNP0C09
that a laptop's firmware reaches its keys, battery and fans through, is
driven as the ACPI specification gives it: its two ports from its
.Ql _CRS ,
its address space for the firmware's methods, installed before the
objects'
.Ql _INI
run, with its
.Ql _REG
run after them, and its general-purpose event from its
.Ql _GPE ,
on which the kernel asks the controller for its event and runs the
method
.Ql _Qxx
it names.
.Pp
The power and sleep buttons and the video bus are input devices, as
.Xr input 4
describes, named as Linux names them.
The FADT's fixed power button and each device
.Ql PNP0C0C
are
.Dq Power Button ,
which reports
.Dv KEY_POWER
for a press or a Notify 0x80; the fixed sleep button and each
.Ql PNP0C0E
are
.Dq Sleep Button ,
with
.Dv KEY_SLEEP .
A firmware may report one press both ways, as two keys.
Each device that has
.Ql _DOD
or
.Ql _DOS ,
such as the graphics device, is a
.Dq Video Bus ,
whose
.Ql _DOS
the kernel calls to keep the firmware from switching outputs, and from
changing the brightness itself when the firmware took Windows 8's path.
A Notify on one of its outputs is a brightness key: 0x85
.Dv KEY_BRIGHTNESS_CYCLE ,
0x86
.Dv KEY_BRIGHTNESSUP ,
0x87
.Dv KEY_BRIGHTNESSDOWN ,
0x88
.Dv KEY_BRIGHTNESS_ZERO
and 0x89
.Dv KEY_DISPLAY_OFF ;
0x80 on the bus itself is
.Dv KEY_SWITCHVIDEOMODE .
The kernel changes no brightness for these keys; user space does, through
.Xr backlight 4 .
.Pp
An IdeaPad's device
.Ql VPC2004 ,
through which its firmware reports the airplane key and others, is
.Dq Ideapad extra buttons ,
with the Wi-Fi switch
.Ql ideapad_wlan ,
as
.Xr ideapad 4
describes.
.Pp
.Pa /proc/acpi/call
evaluates a method by its path for root, as
.Xr proc 5
describes.
.Ql rach2.noacpica
leaves ACPICA out, and
.Ql rach2.nosci
brings it up without a handler for the SCI, whose line then stays masked.
.Pp
Where the MADT lists an I/O APIC, the I/O APIC takes the machine's
interrupt lines 0 to 15 and the 8259 pair is masked.
Each line goes where the MADT's interrupt source override for it says,
with the override's polarity and trigger, or else to the input of its own
number as an ISA line.
A line given in a PCI device's interrupt line register is
level-triggered.
The tick, 100 a second, is then the local APIC's timer, measured at boot
against the HPET, against the ACPI PM timer when there is no HPET, or
against the 8254 when there is neither.
A device that sends messages, MSI or MSI-X, sends them to the local APIC
under either controller.
.Pp
The kernel keeps the 8259 pair and the 8254's tick where the tables list
no I/O APIC, where the machine has no ACPI tables, where the local APIC is
refused, and with
.Ql noapic
or
.Ql maxcpus=0
on the kernel command line.
.Ql rach2.nomsi
refuses the local APIC, and with it messages and the I/O APIC.
.Pp
The local APIC runs in x2APIC mode, its registers model-specific
registers, under a hypervisor that allows it without interrupt remapping,
and, with the x86-64 kernel, on bare metal once VT-d remaps interrupts in
x2APIC format; elsewhere, and with
.Ql nox2apic ,
.Ql noapic
or
.Ql maxcpus=0 ,
it runs in xAPIC mode.
A firmware that left the local APIC in x2APIC mode is the exception: that
mode is kept where the firmware locked it or a hypervisor allows it, under
.Ql noapic
and
.Ql maxcpus=0
too, and
.Ql nox2apic
is ignored where the mode is locked or the processor's identity is 255 or
more.
.Pp
The kernel runs on every processor the MADT lists as enabled, up to 64.
Before the first program it starts each through its local APIC, with INIT
and two startup messages, from code it copies to the page at 0x8000; each
takes its own tick from its local APIC, at the boot processor's measured
rate.
That tick runs out of step with the boot processor's, which alone keeps
time, so another processor reads the place within the boot processor's tick
from the HPET or the PM timer, as the time since that tick began.
.Ql maxcpus=N
limits the processors to N, the boot processor counted; 0 and 1 leave the
kernel on the boot processor.
N is read as Linux reads it: hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x ,
octal after a leading 0, and a value that begins with no digit as 0.
A machine whose local APIC does not tick, that has neither an HPET nor a
PM timer, whose memory map does not give the pages at 0x8000 as free
memory, or whose MADT gives a processor an
APIC ID of 255 or more, which the xAPIC's messages cannot address, runs on
the processors it can start, and the log says why.
The log ends the start with
.Ql smp: processors: N online of M .
.Pp
.Pa /proc/interrupts
gives the controller each vector's interrupts come through in its third
field:
.Ql PIC
for the 8259 pair,
.Ql IO-APIC
for the I/O APIC,
.Ql LAPIC
for the local APIC's timer, whose handler is named
.Ql timer ,
and
.Ql MSI
for a device's message.
Line N arrives on vector 32 + N under either controller.
.Pp
A power off, by
.Xr reboot 2
or
.Xr halt 8 ,
goes through ACPICA where it is up, as Linux's does: the firmware's
.Ql \e_PTS(5)
runs while processes still run, then every general-purpose event is
disabled and the status of every fixed event and general-purpose event
cleared, and the sleep types of
.Ql \e_S5
are written with the sleep enable bit to the FADT's PM1a and PM1b control
registers.
Without ACPICA, or on a machine still running after that, the kernel
writes them itself.
Where the tables give no
.Ql \e_S5 ,
or the machine is still running after the write, the kernel tries the
power controller of the chipsets it knows by name.
A restart writes the FADT's reset value to its reset register where the
FADT says it has one, then tries the firmware's own reset command, the
chipset's reset register at port 0xCF9 and the keyboard controller's
pulse, in that order.
.Ql rach2.resetskip=N
leaves out the first N of these before the keyboard controller's.
.Pp
PCI configuration space is read through the memory window the MCFG gives
for PCI segment 0, on the buses the boot finds behind it, up to 32, and
through ports 0xCF8 and 0xCFC elsewhere.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/proc/interrupts" -compact
.It Pa /proc/interrupts
the vectors that have taken interrupts, as
.Xr proc 5
describes
.It Pa /proc/acpi/call
a method of the firmware's evaluated by root
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The kernel writes these lines to its log, which
.Xr bootlog 4
keeps.
ACPICA's own lines other than its errors and warnings, and the lines of
the SCI, the embedded controller, the video buses and the events, are at
the debug level, which the screen does not show; the serial port has them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql acpi: tables: rsdt at ADDR, revision N, OEM: SIG ...
The root table and the tables that passed their checks.
Lines for the MADT, the FADT,
.Ql \e_S5 ,
the HPET and the MCFG follow.
.It Ql acpi: SIG at ADDR: checksum fails, ignored
A table whose bytes do not sum to zero, left out.
A length that is shorter than the table's fields, or than its header, or
that reaches past the memory the kernel can map, is reported the same
way.
.It Ql ioapic: interrupts: lines through the ioapic, the 8259 masked
The I/O APIC has the lines.
.It Ql lapic: tick: N counts, measured against the hpet
The local APIC's timer is the tick, at N counts a tick;
.Ql the pm timer
or
.Ql the pit
is another reference.
.It Ql lapic: timer: no tick in 50 ms; the pit ticks instead
The local APIC's timer did not interrupt, and the 8254 ticks through the
I/O APIC.
.It Ql pit: none: channel 0 does not count
The machine has no 8254; short waits are measured by the HPET or the PM
timer.
.It Ql acpi: acpica: N objects, E errors
ACPICA is up, with N objects in its namespace; E counts the errors it
logged on the way.
.It Ql acpi: acpica: refused: STEP: EXCEPTION
A step of ACPICA's start failed, and ACPICA stays down.
.It Ql acpi: acpica: skipped: rach2.noacpica
ACPICA was left out.
.It Ql acpi: acpica: TEXT
A line of ACPICA's own log; an error or exception counts in E.
.It Ql acpi: sci: line N
ACPICA's handler took the SCI on line N.
.It Ql acpi: ec: PATH at ports 0xD and 0xC, gpe 0xN
The embedded controller at PATH, with its data and command ports and its
event; its address space handler is in.
.It Ql acpi: ec: gpe 0xN enabled, status 0xS
The controller's event is taken.
.It Ql acpi: ec: timeout: read 0xA, status 0xS
The controller did not answer a read, a write or a query within 500 ms;
the method that asked fails with
.Ql AE_TIME .
The first four are logged.
.It Ql acpi: video: bus PATH, outputs N
A video bus, with the N outputs whose Notify is taken.
.It Ql acpi: event: power button
The fixed power button was pressed;
.Ql acpi: event: power button: notify
is a Notify 0x80 on a power button device, and
.Ql sleep button
the same of a sleep button.
.It Ql acpi: event: video bus: output notify 0xV: key K
A video bus's Notify reported as key K.
.It Ql acpi: call: PATH: RESULT
A write of
.Pa /proc/acpi/call
evaluated PATH.
.It Ql poweroff: acpica: \e_PTS(5) run, gpes disabled
The power off ran the firmware's preparation through ACPICA.
.It Ql poweroff: acpica: status cleared
ACPICA cleared every status and is writing the sleep enable bit.
.It Ql poweroff: acpi: sleep types A and B
The power off writes
.Ql \e_S5 's
types itself, without ACPICA.
.It Ql reboot: acpi: writing 0xV to the reset register
The restart writes the FADT's reset register.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr reboot 2 ,
.Xr backlight 4 ,
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr ideapad 4 ,
.Xr input 4 ,
.Xr nvme 4 ,
.Xr rtc 4 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr halt 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The kernel does not ask ACPICA for a PCI device's interrupt routing,
.Ql _PRT .
A device without MSI is served on the ISA line its interrupt line
register gives; one whose interrupt reaches only the I/O APIC's inputs
above 15 takes no interrupts, and only its driver's polling serves it.
.Pp
Of the general-purpose events, only the embedded controller's and those
the firmware gave a method are enabled.
An embedded controller whose
.Ql _GPE
is a package, naming a GPE block device, takes no events, and only the
first controller is driven.
.Pp
The other processors the MADT lists enabled are started and take work, up
to the kernel's 64 and the
.Ql maxcpus
the boot line gives.
